:title("C (subset) interpreter") ;
:description("Parses a C subset and executes it directly, without generating code.

The language is a real C subset, richer than TinyC: the types int, int* and int[N]
(globals and locals, globals with constant initializers), structs (struct definitions
with int, pointer and nested struct value fields - including pointers to the struct
itself for linked lists -, struct locals, globals and arrays of structs, struct
pointers with initializers, . and -> member access along whole paths like
nodes[i].next->value, &s and struct pointer parameters), address-of (&x, &a[i]),
dereferencing (*p, also as assignment target), pointer arithmetic (p + i steps whole
ints), pre and post ++/--, assignment as an expression with = += -= *= /= %=, the
ternary operator, || and && with real C short circuit evaluation, the bitwise operators
| ^ & << >> and unary - ! ~, char literals ('A', '\n'), comparisons, function calls and
recursion, switch with real C fallthrough (stacked case labels, default, break), and
putchar()/getchar(). int is 32 bit, / and % truncate towards zero.
unions (every member at offset 0, sizeof the widest one), bit fields and anonymous
struct/union members, brace initializers for structs and unions with .field designators,
and tag definitions that also declare objects (struct S { int x; } s;).
Arrays of any rank (int m[2][3]) with nested brace initializers and [i] designators,
pointers to arrays (int (*pa)[3]) and arrays of pointers; struct VALUES - copied on
initialization and assignment, passed to and returned from functions - and struct
members of array type; function pointers (declared, stored in arrays, passed, returned
and called, including through an expression like tab[0](3) and pick(1)(5)); typedef,
including array, struct and function-pointer typedefs; and compound literals
((int[]){ 1, 2, 3 }, and the address of one).

A real TYPE MODEL carries every expression: char, short, int and long with their own
widths, signed and unsigned as distinct types, float and double, pointers, arrays,
structs and function pointers. Every value is the number plus the C type it has, so
the usual arithmetic conversions pick the common type of a binary operator, an
unsigned divide, remainder, shift and comparison differ from the signed one, 0u - 1u
wraps to 4294967295 where 0 - 1 is -1, a cast really converts, sizeof answers the size
of what it is applied to (including a whole array or struct), string literals are char
arrays that can be indexed and measured, and _Generic selects on the type.
long and long long are REAL 64-bit types (LP64, like cc on this host): their literals,
arithmetic, wrap-around, shifts and unsigned comparisons all run through the
language-neutral sized-integer layer of lib/interp-core.js, so 5000000000L keeps every
bit and (unsigned long)-1 is 18446744073709551615. A declared return type converts the
returned value, so a function declared int truncates a long.
Not in the subset: aggregate initializers on globals, and variable-length arrays.

The interpreter uses one flat, word addressed memory array: every variable lives at an
address, scopes map names to addresses, and pointers are plain indexes; every int,
pointer and struct field takes one word. That makes the semantics exactly match the
compiled version (c-to-llvm-ir.abnf), where the same programs run as LLVM IR.

Every tag composes a small JS closure (a 'thunk') out of the thunks of its children.
Lvalue thunks return an address, expression thunks return the value, statement thunks
return undefined or a signal (return/break/continue) that the enclosing statements route.

The exit code of the metacompiler run is the value that main() returns, so C test
programs are self checking: they return 0 on success.") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
:include("lib/cstyle-comments.abnf") ;
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

Program     = { TopItem } ;
TopItem     = Include | PPCond | PPLine | StaticAssert | EnumDef | UnionDef | UnionGDecl | TagSkip
              | TypedefDecl
              | StructDef | GStructDefDecl | GUnionDefDecl | TagFwd | StructGDecl
              | FnPtrFuncDef | TdProto | TdFuncDef | TdGlobalDecl | SProto | SFuncDef | EFuncDef | EProto | FuncDef | Proto | GlobalDecl ;

// Preprocessor directives other than #include are accepted and skipped (object-like
// #define, the #if/#ifdef/#ifndef/#else/#elif/#endif family, #undef, #pragma, #error,
// #line). The line (with backslash continuations) is consumed as text and ignored;
// macro bodies are NOT expanded, so a name #define'd to a value stays unresolved if
// used later. Include is tried first, so #include keeps its header resolution.
PPLine      = "#" :whitespace() { PPCont | PPChar } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PPCont      = "\\" [ "\r" ] "\n" ;
PPChar      = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// enum Color { RED, GREEN = 5, BLUE }; -- genuinely lowered to named int constants
// (sequential from 0, or from an explicit value). The optional tag is ignored; the
// constants live in a global table and resolve wherever a bare name is read.
EnumDef     <~~ defineEnum(takeAll()) ~~>
            = KwEnum [ Id <~~ push({tag: up.in}) ~~> ]
              EnumOpen [ Enumerator { "," Enumerator } [ "," ] ] "}" ";" ;
// The running enumerator counter is per enum BODY, so it restarts at the brace.
EnumOpen    <~~ enumStart() ~~> = "{" ;
Enumerator  <~~ push(makeEnumerator(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ "=" EnumInit ] ;
// An enum initializer is a constant EXPRESSION, and it may NAME another enum constant:
// 'enum c_tree_code { C_DUMMY = LAST_AND_UNUSED_TREE_CODE, ... }' continues a previous
// enum's numbering, which is how the GCC tree-code enums are chained. The value is
// folded here, at parse time, exactly like an array dimension - by the time an
// enumerator reduces, every constant before it (this enum's included, see
// makeEnumerator) is already in the table, so the fold never needs a frame.
EnumInit    <~~ push({val: pop()}) ~~> = ECShift ;
ECShift     <~~ push(foldDimBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ECAdd { DimShOp ECAdd } ;
ECAdd       <~~ push(foldDimBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ECMul { DimAddOp ECMul } ;
ECMul       <~~ push(foldDimBin(takeAll())) ~~> = ECUn { DimMulOp ECUn } ;
ECUn        = Number | ECParen | ECNeg | ECPos | ECNot | ECName ;
ECParen     = "(" ECShift ")" ;
ECNeg       <~~ push(0 - pop()) ~~> = "-" ECUn ;
ECPos       = "+" ECUn ;
ECNot       <~~ push(0 - pop() - 1) ~~> = "~" ECUn ;
ECName      <~~ push(enumConstOf(up.in)) ~~> = Id ;

// union U { int i; unsigned u; }; -- a union is laid out exactly like a struct whose
// fields ALL sit at offset 0, with the size (and the byte size behind sizeof) the
// maximum over the members. Tags live in the same table as the struct tags but under
// a 'u:' prefixed key, so 'struct X' and 'union X' stay distinct C tag namespaces.
UnionDef    <~~ defineUnion(takeAll()) ~~>
            = KwUnion { Attr } Id <~~ push(uKey(up.in)) ~~> "{" { FieldLine } "}" { Attr } ";" ;
// Global union variables, pointers and arrays, with the same declarator syntax as
// everything else - the tag only supplies the base type.
UnionGDecl  <~~ defineGlobals(takeAll()) ~~>
            = [ TypeInt ] KwUnion Id <~~ push({bty: ctStruct(uKey(up.in))}) ~~> [ TypeInt ] DeclItem { "," DeclItem } ";" ;
// An ANONYMOUS struct or union at file scope introduces no tag and no object, so
// it declares nothing at all - cc says "declaration does not declare anything" and
// compiles it. It is accepted and ignored. A tagged definition and a definition that
// also declares objects both keep their own productions: SkipBlock has to start at
// the '{' and the ';' has to follow the '}', so neither shape can reach here.
TagSkip     = ( KwUnion | KwStruct ) SkipBlock ";" ;

// typedef: the introduced name becomes a real type name, usable wherever a
// declaration base type may stand. Because a PEG guard cannot consult a symbol
// table, the USE site is recognized by SHAPE - an identifier that is not a C
// keyword, directly followed by a declarator - and the tag resolves the name in
// the typedef table it filled here. That covers the ordinary forms:
//   typedef int Int;  typedef Int Num;  typedef int Tri[3];
//   typedef struct { int v; } Box;  typedef int (*Fn)(int);
TypedefDecl <~~ defineTypedef(takeAll()) ~~>
            = KwTypedef TdBase TdDeclr { "," TdDeclr } ";" ;
// The tag-DEFINING forms come first: only what follows the tag tells
// 'typedef struct S { ... } T;' from 'typedef struct S T;' apart.
TdBase      = TdStructDef | TdUnionDef | TdEnumDef
            | TypeInt <~~ push({tb: baseCtOf(up.in)}) ~~>
            | KwStruct Id <~~ push({tb: ctStruct(up.in)}) ~~>
            | KwUnion Id <~~ push({tb: ctStruct(uKey(up.in))}) ~~>
            | EnumRef <~~ push({tb: ctInt}) ~~>
            | TdRef ;
TdRef       <~~ push({tref: up.in}) ~~> = NotCKw Id ;
TdStructDef <~~ push({tb: tdTagDef(takeAll(), false)}) ~~>
            = KwStruct { Attr } [ Id <~~ push({tag: up.in}) ~~> ] "{" { FieldLine } "}" { Attr } ;
TdUnionDef  <~~ push({tb: tdTagDef(takeAll(), true)}) ~~>
            = KwUnion { Attr } [ Id <~~ push({tag: up.in}) ~~> ] "{" { FieldLine } "}" { Attr } ;
TdEnumDef   <~~ defineEnum(takeAll()); push({tb: ctInt}) ~~>
            = KwEnum [ Id <~~ push({tag: up.in}) ~~> ]
              EnumOpen [ Enumerator { "," Enumerator } [ "," ] ] "}" ;
TdDeclr     = TdFnPtr | TdPlain ;
TdFnPtr     <~~ push({dcl: makeDeclr(takeAll(), "fnptr")}) ~~>
            = [ PStars ] "(" Stars Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ArrDims ] ")" FnParams ;
TdPlain     <~~ push({dcl: makeDeclr(takeAll(), "plain")}) ~~>
            = [ Stars ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ArrDims ] ;

// A declaration (or a function definition) whose base type is a typedef name.
TdDecl      <~~ push(makeDeclSeq(tdBase(takeAll()), false)) ~~>
            = TdName DeclItem { "," DeclItem } ";" ;
TdFuncDef   <~~ defineTdFunc(takeAll()) ~~>
            = TdName [ PStars ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ParamList { Attr } Block ;
TdProto     <~~ takeAll() /* A prototype needs no lowering. */ ~~>
            = TdName [ Stars ] Id ParamList { Attr } ";" ;
// A file-scope object whose base type is a typedef name.
TdGlobalDecl <~~ defineGlobals(tdBase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TdName DeclItem { "," DeclItem } ";" ;
TdName      <~~ push({tname: up.in}) ~~> = NotCKw Id ;

// An identifier that is not one of C's own keywords. A typedef USE is recognized
// by shape alone, so without this guard 'return x;' would read as a declaration.
NotCKw = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var s = 0 // Neither whitespace NOR COMMENTS are skipped before a :script.
        while (true) {
            var ch = c.peek(s)
            if (ch === 32 || ch === 9 || ch === 10 || ch === 13 || ch === 12) { s++; continue }
            if (ch === 47 && c.peek(s + 1) === 47) {
                s += 2
                while (c.peek(s) >= 0 && c.peek(s) !== 10) { s++ }
                continue
            }
            if (ch === 47 && c.peek(s + 1) === 42) {
                s += 2
                while (c.peek(s) >= 0 && !(c.peek(s) === 42 && c.peek(s + 1) === 47)) { s++ }
                s += 2
                continue
            }
            break
        }
        var kws = ["auto", "break", "case", "char", "const", "continue", "default", "do",
                   "double", "else", "enum", "extern", "float", "for", "goto", "if",
                   "inline", "int", "long", "register", "restrict", "return", "short",
                   "signed", "sizeof", "static", "struct", "switch", "typedef", "union",
                   "unsigned", "void", "volatile", "while", "_Alignas", "_Alignof",
                   "_Atomic", "_Bool", "_Complex", "_Generic", "_Noreturn",
                   "_Static_assert", "_Thread_local"]
        var hit = false
        for (var k = 0; k < kws.length; k++) {
            var w = kws[k]
            var ok = true
            for (var i = 0; i < w.length; i++) { if (c.peek(s + i) !== w.charCodeAt(i)) { ok = false } }
            if (ok) {
                var cc = c.peek(s + w.length)
                if (!((cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || cc === 95 || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122))) { hit = true }
            }
        }
        return hit ? abnf.newToken("\x01c-keyword", 0) : undefined
    })()
~~) ;


// ----- Conditional compilation -----
// #if / #ifdef / #ifndef ... #elif ... #else ... #endif. Only the LIVE branch is
// parsed as source; every dead branch is consumed as raw lines, so no tag of a dead
// branch ever runs - which is the whole difficulty, because this grammar's top level
// EXECUTES as it walks (see docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md). The macros a condition
// reads are the object-like #define / #undef lines before it; the full expansion of
// macro BODIES is a separate pass (languages/c-preprocessor.abnf, -pipe).
PPCond      = PPCondFalse PPIfLine PPDeadBody PPDeadChain
            | PPIfLine { PPBodyItem } PPAfterLive ;
// Nothing has been taken yet, so an #else here is live.
PPDeadChain = PPEndifLine
            | PPCondFalse PPElifLine PPDeadBody PPDeadChain
            | PPElifLine { PPBodyItem } PPAfterLive
            | PPElseLine { PPBodyItem } PPEndifLine ;
// A branch WAS taken, so every later branch is dead whatever its condition says.
PPAfterLive = PPEndifLine
            | PPMidLine PPDeadBody PPAfterLive ;
PPMidLine   = PPElifLine | PPElseLine ;
// An item of a LIVE branch. It may open a nested conditional, but it must not run
// into the #elif / #else / #endif that ends the branch it is in - PPLine would
// happily skip that as just another directive line.
PPBodyItem  = NotPPEnd TopItem ;
PPBodyStmt  = NotPPEnd Statement ;
PPDeadBody  = { PPNestCond | PPDeadLine } ;
PPNestCond  = PPIfLine PPDeadBody PPAfterLive ;
// One line of a dead branch, consumed as text. The first character is matched with
// whitespace skipping in force, so empty lines and comments are stepped over; the
// guard runs before that and looks at the line the parser will actually reach.
PPDeadLine  = NotPPBranch PPDeadTxt ;
PPDeadTxt   = PPChar :whitespace() { PPChar } "\n" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PPIfLine    = "#" :whitespace() { PPSp } PPIfKw { PPCont | PPChar } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PPElifLine  = "#" :whitespace() { PPSp } "elif" { PPCont | PPChar } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PPElseLine  = "#" :whitespace() { PPSp } "else" { PPCont | PPChar } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PPEndifLine = "#" :whitespace() { PPSp } "endif" { PPCont | PPChar } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PPIfKw      = "ifdef" | "ifndef" | "if" ;
PPSp        = " " | "\t" ;

// The guard that decides a conditional: it SUCCEEDS when the directive's condition
// is FALSE. That is what makes the dead reading the FIRST alternative of PPCond and
// the live one what is left, so a live body that does not parse stays a parse error
// instead of being silently swallowed as dead text. Being a guard it consumes
// nothing, and being pure it re-derives the macro table from the text before it on
// every call (a :script cannot see the tag scripts' state, and must not keep any).
PPCondFalse = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var CAP = 500000
        var pos = 0
        function isIdCh(cc) { return (cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90) || cc === 95 || (cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) }
        function isSp(cc) { return cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 }
        function skipSp() { while (isSp(c.peek(pos))) { pos = pos + 1 } }
        function wordAt(p, w) {
            for (var k = 0; k < w.length; k++) { if (c.peek(p + k) !== w.charCodeAt(k)) { return false } }
            return !isIdCh(c.peek(p + w.length))
        }
        function nameLenAt(p) { var n = 0; while (isIdCh(c.peek(p + n))) { n = n + 1 } return n }
        function sameId(a, alen, b) {
            if (nameLenAt(b) !== alen) { return false }
            for (var k = 0; k < alen; k++) { if (c.peek(a + k) !== c.peek(b + k)) { return false } }
            return true
        }
        // An integer constant at p (decimal or hexadecimal); 0 when there is none.
        function intAt(p) {
            var q = p
            var base = 10
            if (c.peek(q) === 48 && (c.peek(q + 1) === 120 || c.peek(q + 1) === 88)) { base = 16; q = q + 2 }
            var v = 0
            while (true) {
                var d = -1
                var cc = c.peek(q)
                if (cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) { d = cc - 48 }
                else if (base === 16 && cc >= 97 && cc <= 102) { d = cc - 87 }
                else if (base === 16 && cc >= 65 && cc <= 70) { d = cc - 55 }
                if (d < 0 || d >= base) { break }
                v = v * base + d
                q = q + 1
            }
            return v
        }
        // [defined, value] of one macro, from every #define / #undef before this
        // directive - the last one that mentions the name wins.
        function macroOf(at, alen) {
            var st = 0
            var g = 0
            while (g < CAP && c.peek(st - 1) >= 0) { st = st - 1; g = g + 1 }
            var def = 0
            var val = 0
            var p = st
            g = 0
            while (p < 0 && g < CAP) {
                g = g + 1
                var q = p
                while (isSp(c.peek(q))) { q = q + 1 }
                if (c.peek(q) === 35) {
                    var d = q + 1
                    while (isSp(c.peek(d))) { d = d + 1 }
                    var isD = wordAt(d, "define")
                    var isU = wordAt(d, "undef")
                    if (isD || isU) {
                        var n = isD ? d + 6 : d + 5
                        while (isSp(c.peek(n))) { n = n + 1 }
                        if (sameId(at, alen, n)) {
                            if (isU) { def = 0; val = 0 }
                            else {
                                def = 1
                                var v2 = n + alen
                                while (isSp(c.peek(v2))) { v2 = v2 + 1 }
                                val = intAt(v2)
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
                while (p < 0 && c.peek(p) !== 10) { p = p + 1 }
                p = p + 1
            }
            return [def, val]
        }
        // The condition expression, by precedence. An undefined identifier is 0,
        // which is what the standard says a #if sees.
        function primary() {
            skipSp()
            var cc = c.peek(pos)
            if (cc === 33) { pos = pos + 1; return primary() === 0 ? 1 : 0 }
            if (cc === 40) {
                pos = pos + 1
                var v = orE()
                skipSp()
                if (c.peek(pos) === 41) { pos = pos + 1 }
                return v
            }
            if (cc === 45) { pos = pos + 1; return 0 - primary() }
            if (cc === 43) { pos = pos + 1; return primary() }
            if (cc >= 48 && cc <= 57) {
                var n = intAt(pos)
                while (isIdCh(c.peek(pos))) { pos = pos + 1 }
                return n
            }
            if (!isIdCh(cc)) { return 0 }
            if (wordAt(pos, "defined")) {
                pos = pos + 7
                skipSp()
                var paren = false
                if (c.peek(pos) === 40) { paren = true; pos = pos + 1; skipSp() }
                var a = pos
                var al = nameLenAt(a)
                pos = pos + al
                if (paren) { skipSp(); if (c.peek(pos) === 41) { pos = pos + 1 } }
                return macroOf(a, al)[0]
            }
            var b = pos
            var bl = nameLenAt(b)
            pos = pos + bl
            return macroOf(b, bl)[1]
        }
        function mulE() {
            var v = primary()
            while (true) {
                skipSp()
                var cc = c.peek(pos)
                if (cc === 42) { pos = pos + 1; v = v * primary(); continue }
                if (cc === 47) { pos = pos + 1; var r = primary(); v = (r === 0) ? 0 : ((v / r) | 0); continue }
                if (cc === 37) { pos = pos + 1; var r2 = primary(); v = (r2 === 0) ? 0 : (v % r2); continue }
                break
            }
            return v
        }
        function addE() {
            var v = mulE()
            while (true) {
                skipSp()
                var cc = c.peek(pos)
                if (cc === 43) { pos = pos + 1; v = v + mulE(); continue }
                if (cc === 45) { pos = pos + 1; v = v - mulE(); continue }
                break
            }
            return v
        }
        function shE() {
            var v = addE()
            while (true) {
                skipSp()
                var cc = c.peek(pos)
                var c2 = c.peek(pos + 1)
                if (cc === 60 && c2 === 60) { pos = pos + 2; v = v << addE(); continue }
                if (cc === 62 && c2 === 62) { pos = pos + 2; v = v >> addE(); continue }
                break
            }
            return v
        }
        function relE() {
            var v = shE()
            while (true) {
                skipSp()
                var cc = c.peek(pos)
                var c2 = c.peek(pos + 1)
                if (cc === 60 && c2 === 61) { pos = pos + 2; v = (v <= shE()) ? 1 : 0; continue }
                if (cc === 62 && c2 === 61) { pos = pos + 2; v = (v >= shE()) ? 1 : 0; continue }
                if (cc === 60 && c2 !== 60) { pos = pos + 1; v = (v < shE()) ? 1 : 0; continue }
                if (cc === 62 && c2 !== 62) { pos = pos + 1; v = (v > shE()) ? 1 : 0; continue }
                break
            }
            return v
        }
        function eqE() {
            var v = relE()
            while (true) {
                skipSp()
                var cc = c.peek(pos)
                var c2 = c.peek(pos + 1)
                if (cc === 61 && c2 === 61) { pos = pos + 2; v = (v === relE()) ? 1 : 0; continue }
                if (cc === 33 && c2 === 61) { pos = pos + 2; v = (v !== relE()) ? 1 : 0; continue }
                break
            }
            return v
        }
        function andE() {
            var v = eqE()
            while (true) {
                skipSp()
                if (c.peek(pos) === 38 && c.peek(pos + 1) === 38) { pos = pos + 2; var r = eqE(); v = (v !== 0 && r !== 0) ? 1 : 0; continue }
                break
            }
            return v
        }
        function orE() {
            var v = andE()
            while (true) {
                skipSp()
                if (c.peek(pos) === 124 && c.peek(pos + 1) === 124) { pos = pos + 2; var r = andE(); v = (v !== 0 || r !== 0) ? 1 : 0; continue }
                break
            }
            return v
        }
        // Which directive is here? Whitespace AND comments are still ahead.
        var i = 0
        while (true) {
            var ch = c.peek(i)
            if (ch === 32 || ch === 9 || ch === 10 || ch === 13 || ch === 12) { i = i + 1; continue }
            if (ch === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47) {
                i = i + 2
                while (c.peek(i) >= 0 && c.peek(i) !== 10) { i = i + 1 }
                continue
            }
            if (ch === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 42) {
                i = i + 2
                while (c.peek(i) >= 0 && !(c.peek(i) === 42 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47)) { i = i + 1 }
                i = i + 2
                continue
            }
            break
        }
        if (c.peek(i) !== 35) { return abnf.newToken("\x01not-a-directive", 0) }
        var dd = i + 1
        while (isSp(c.peek(dd))) { dd = dd + 1 }
        var res = -1
        if (wordAt(dd, "ifdef")) {
            pos = dd + 5
            skipSp()
            res = macroOf(pos, nameLenAt(pos))[0]
        } else if (wordAt(dd, "ifndef")) {
            pos = dd + 6
            skipSp()
            res = (macroOf(pos, nameLenAt(pos))[0] === 0) ? 1 : 0
        } else if (wordAt(dd, "if")) {
            pos = dd + 2
            res = (orE() === 0) ? 0 : 1
        } else if (wordAt(dd, "elif")) {
            pos = dd + 4
            res = (orE() === 0) ? 0 : 1
        }
        if (res < 0) { return abnf.newToken("\x01not-a-conditional", 0) }
        return (res === 0) ? undefined : abnf.newToken("\x01condition-holds", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// A line inside a dead branch must not be one of the directives that structure the
// conditional: those belong to the productions around it.
NotPPBranch = :script(~~
    (function () {
        var s = 0
        while (true) {
            var ch = c.peek(s)
            if (ch === 32 || ch === 9 || ch === 10 || ch === 13 || ch === 12) { s = s + 1; continue }
            if (ch === 47 && c.peek(s + 1) === 47) {
                s = s + 2
                while (c.peek(s) >= 0 && c.peek(s) !== 10) { s = s + 1 }
                continue
            }
            if (ch === 47 && c.peek(s + 1) === 42) {
                s = s + 2
                while (c.peek(s) >= 0 && !(c.peek(s) === 42 && c.peek(s + 1) === 47)) { s = s + 1 }
                s = s + 2
                continue
            }
            break
        }
